= Computability — Simple =

'''Computability''' studies which problems can be solved by an algorithm at all.

Some problems have algorithms.

Other precisely defined problems do not have any general algorithm that always gives the correct answer.

The halting problem is a famous example of an undecidable problem.

Computability is different from speed.

A problem can be computable but still take far too much time or memory to solve in practice.

That practical-resource question belongs to computational complexity.

